            Desc2    = "MT4 supports nine timeframes (M1 through MN), 30 built-in technical indicators, three chart types, and a comprehensive strategy tester for backtesting EAs on historical data. The platform runs on Windows, macOS (via Wine or CrossOver), iOS, Android, and in-browser via WebTrader.",
            Pros     = new[] { "Largest EA and indicator library in the industry", "Widely supported by almost every forex broker", "Familiar, stable interface with 20+ years of refinement", "Excellent for automated trading with MQL4 EAs", "Free via your broker — no subscription cost", "Strong community and support resources" },
            Cons     = new[] { "Limited to 9 timeframes vs MT5's 21", "No built-in economic calendar or news feed integration", "Older technology — lacks multi-threading and faster execution", "Cannot hedge on same account with some brokers", "MQL4 becoming outdated compared to MQL5", "Limited asset classes — primarily forex and CFDs" }
        },
        new {
            Id       = "mt5",
            Name     = "MetaTrader 5",
            Short    = "MT5",
            Year     = "2010",
            Dev      = "MetaQuotes Software",
            Lang     = "MQL5",
            TF       = "21 timeframes",
            Ind      = "38 built-in",
            EA       = "Full EA + MQL5",
            BestFor  = "Multi-asset, Stocks, Futures, Algo",
            Icon     = "fa-solid fa-chart-bar",
            Color    = "#16a34a",
            Bg       = "#f0fdf4",
            Border   = "#bbf7d0",
            Tag      = "Next Generation",
            TagBg    = "#16a34a",
            Desc     = "MetaTrader 5 was released in 2010 as the successor to MT4, offering substantially more timeframes (21 vs 9), more built-in indicators (38 vs 30), improved backtesting with multi-currency optimisation, and support for additional asset classes including stocks, futures, and options. MQL5 is a more modern, object-oriented language than MQL4 with better performance and broader capabilities.",
            Desc2    = "MT5 also supports the MetaTrader Market — a marketplace for EAs, indicators, and scripts — and integrates an economic calendar directly into the platform. Hedging is supported by default on MT5, making it the preferred choice for traders who want to hold opposing positions simultaneously. Execution speed improvements and multi-threading make it faster for HFT and high-volume strategies.",
            Pros     = new[] { "21 timeframes including M2, M3, M4, M6, M10, M12, M20 not on MT4", "More built-in indicators and chart types", "Superior backtester with multi-currency optimisation", "Supports stocks, futures, options alongside forex/CFDs", "Built-in economic calendar with news feed", "MQL5 is more powerful and modern than MQL4" },
            Cons     = new[] { "Smaller EA library than MT4 (MQL4 EAs don't work on MT5)", "Less universally supported than MT4 by brokers", "Migration from MT4 requires rewriting EAs in MQL5", "Some traders find it more complex than MT4", "Not all MT4 indicators are compatible without conversion", "Larger installation size and resource usage" }
        },
